<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free State of Lippe</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1947 CE · capital Detmold</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Westphalian republic whose January 1933 election the Nazis used as a showcase just before Hitler came to power.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-iv-prince-of-lippe">Prince Leopold IV</a> abdicated on 12 November 1918, and Lippe became a free state with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detmold">Detmold</a> as capital. A constitution adopted in 1920 established a parliamentary government with a small Landtag.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Lippe was rural, with small farms, furniture making and a tradition of seasonal brickmakers. Social Democrats and liberals generally governed. In the Landtag election of 15 January 1933 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazis</a> concentrated their campaign on the tiny state, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Hitler</a> speaking repeatedly, and their gain of votes was presented as proof of momentum two weeks before he became chancellor. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazis</a> Lippe shared a Reich governor with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-schaumburg-lippe">Schaumburg-Lippe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-meyer">Alfred Meyer</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1945 Lippe was in the British zone. Rather than joining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, it opted in January 1947 to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, under guarantees known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lippe-punctations">Lippe Punctations</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-state-of-lippe.jpg" alt="Free State of Lippe" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_F%C3%BCrstentum_Lippe.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1947 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Detmold</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Heinrich Drake</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Freistaat Lippe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1932&amp;focus=free-state-of-lippe" aria-label="Free State of Lippe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4880 382 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free State of Lippe lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5200 638L5200 638M5200 638L5300 622M5200 638L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5200 638h0M5300 622h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5200 638h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5200 638h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Detmold. <a href="/atlas?year=1932&amp;focus=free-state-of-lippe">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
